When a great drought came, Aria's river began to dry. The Sky Singer descended and said, "Your vessels are beautiful, but they hold nothing. What good is beauty without purpose?"

Aria understood. She carried her finest vessels—seven in all—to the riverbed. One by one, she broke them against the stones, and from each shattered piece, something miraculous emerged. From the first came flowers, their petals the colors of her joy. From the second, flowing water returned. From the third, fruit-bearing trees. From the fourth, singing birds. From the fifth, gentle rain. From the sixth, rich soil. And from the seventh, seeds of every kind.

"You have given your art meaning through sacrifice," the Sky Singer said. "Now your spirit lives in every growing thing."

Aria smiled, her hands forever empty but her heart full. And to this day, every garden remembers the potter who broke her most precious creations to feed the world, proving that true beauty serves others.
